#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088947 :tutorial/content "Recall that a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation takes the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $c(x)$ are functions of $x$ only and $f(x)$ is a nonzero function.\n\nIn general, we say that a second-order ODE has **exponential forcing** if $f(x)$ is an exponential function. For example, the ODE below has exponential forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =10 e ^ {2x}\n$$\n\nThe method for solving a second-order ODE with exponential forcing is similar to the method for solving a second-order ODE with polynomial forcing. The general solution is still the sum of the complementary solution $y_c$ (i.e., the solution to the associated homogeneous ODE) and a particular solution $y_p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nThe only difference is in how we find a particular solution. Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ provided that $e^{bx}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{bx}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a known constant and $\\alpha$ is an unknown constant to be determined." :tutorial/id #uuid "a08b4b47-ae27-534c-b39b-1696d513b79e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4063 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088948 :tutorial/content "Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ if $e^{bx}$ *is* a term in the complementary solution, then $\\alpha e^{bx}$ *cannot* be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e^{bx}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =3 e ^ {-2x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +3) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = -2$ or $\\lambda = - 3.$ Consequently, the complimentary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution to the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $3e^{-2x},$ and $e^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}+\\alpha {e}^{-2x}, \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =4\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}-4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}}_{―}-4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+5({-2\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}}_{―}+\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+{6\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}}_{―} & =3{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCanceling the underlined terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = 3 x e^{-2x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+3x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "76ee6f3c-a454-54c3-8075-56c1c0a27261" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4064 :tutorial/title "The Case When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088949 :tutorial/content "Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ if $e^{bx}$ and $xe^{bx}$ are *both* terms in the complementary solution, then neither can be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{bx}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 36y = e ^{6x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda -6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 6$ is a repeated root. Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 x} + Bxe^{6 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{6x},$ and $e^{6x}$ and $xe^{6x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =6\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =12x\\alpha {e}^{6x}+36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+12\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\  & =36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+24\\alpha x{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\underline {36 \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}}\n+2 \\alpha e ^{6x} +\\underline{ 24 \\alpha x e ^{6x} }-12(\\underline{6 \\alpha x^ 2e ^{6x} }+ \n\\underline {2 \\alpha x e^{6x}})\n+\\underline{36  \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}} = e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nCanceling the underlined terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x}\\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2} x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{c} \\\\  & =A{e}^{6x}+Bx{e}^{6x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "59c57ff3-a1a4-5ed4-be1c-15350d769274" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4065 :tutorial/title "Second-Order ODEs With Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088954 :tutorial/content "Let's solve the following second-order ODE with exponential forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =10 e ^ {2x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation (or auxiliary equation) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = -3$ or $\\lambda = -2.$ Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $10e^{2x},$ and $e^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2 \\alpha e ^{2x},\n\\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 4  \\alpha e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+6\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ 20\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf4c356b-2a4a-5811-905f-b7e539bdc8d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4138 :tutorial/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"}]}
